  {
   "id": "DlX8o3b1AAAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2020-04-23T06:05:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: standardisation: difference between summary's Estimate and std.all when fit(..., std.ov=TRUE, std.lv=TRUE)",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I fail to understand why, when I specified std.ov=TRUE and <a href=\"http://std.lv\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421971175000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1OiHYy0lCO6yFsdYMBiRnJ\">std.lv</a>=TRUE in the fit function, the summary&#39;s <b>std.all and estimates</b> are different. </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>If you are asking why the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">std.all</font> in the first example does not match the unstandardized estimated from the second, it is because your latent variables are not all exogenous.  <font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><a href=\"http://std.lv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421971175000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1OiHYy0lCO6yFsdYMBiRnJ\">std.lv</a>=TRUE</font> sets the variances in Psi to 1 for identification, but those are residual variances (only coincidentally total variances when latents are exogenous, like <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">ind60</font>).  If you compare them instead using the<font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> ?cfa </font>example, you will see the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">std.all</font> in the first example does match the unstandardized estimated (as well as the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">std.all</font> column) from the second.</div><div><br></div><div>But if you merely compare the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">std.all</font> columns from both of your examples, they should match (they do when I run your syntax).  But I think your output does not match because the first example seems to additionally include <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">x1</font> as a predictor of <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">dem65</font>, which changes the meaning of the other partial regression coefficients. </div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421971175000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1yZaSc3hjMZswtQ65pT0dr\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
